gbh2(){return"Why does it work?"},
gbh8(){return"A Stanford study (Balban et al., 2023) found that 5 minutes a day of sighing improved mood and slowed breathing more than meditating."},
gbh7(){return"Exhaling for longer than you inhale engages the heart's natural brake (the vagus nerve). Review: Zaccaro et al., 2018."},
gbh5(){return"About 6 breaths per minute raise heart rate variability. A meta-analysis (Lehrer et al., 2020) links it with less stress when practiced regularly."},
gbh4(){return"The slow, even rhythm helps you stay calm under pressure, and emergency teams use it. It has fewer studies of its own than the others."},
gbh3(){return"The long exhale helps you let go. It has few studies of its own (Vierra et al., 2022, saw heart rate drop). If holding feels uncomfortable, use Long exhale."},
gbh6(){return"Fast breathing with holds, Wim Hof style, raises adrenaline for a while (Kox et al., 2014). It energizes, it doesn't calm."},
