gaEV(){return"Length"},
gaOI(){return"A way to get through a strong urge without fighting it or giving in. You watch it rise, peak and fall, like a wave."},
gaOz(){return"5 or 10 minutes"},
gaOJ(){return"You have a craving: food, smoking, drinking or buying something."},
gaOK(){return"You want to open your phone or an app without thinking."},
gaOL(){return"You feel an urge you usually regret afterwards."},
gaOB(){return"Rate the urge"},
gaOA(){return"From 1 to 10, how strong it is now. It helps you compare at the end."},
gaOD(){return"Find where you feel it"},
gaOC(){return"In your mouth, chest, stomach or hands. Just notice it, you don't have to change anything."},
gaOF(){return"Watch it rise and fall"},
gaOE(){return"A timer runs and the screen tells you what to notice at each moment. Breathe normally and let the wave pass."},
gaOH(){return"Rate it again"},
gaOG(){return"At the end you rate it again and see if it changed. If it's still high, add 5 more minutes."},
gaOM(){return"Urges rise, reach a high point and then fall, even if you do nothing. Watching them without fighting or giving in helps you get through them. In a study with smokers, those who learned this technique smoked less the following week (Bowen and Marlatt, 2009)."},
gcgS(){return"How strong is the urge right now? From 1 to 10 (optional)"},
gcgQ(){return"And now, how strong is it?"},
cgT(a,b){return"You started at "+a+" and now you are at "+b+"."},
gcgR(){return"It dropped. That's how urges behave: they rise and pass."},
gcgV(){return"About the same. If it rises again, you can watch it again."},
gcgU(){return"Still high. Give it 5 more minutes, the wave is still passing."},
gaMB(){return"Cold on your face for 30 seconds. It triggers a body reflex that slows your heart rate within seconds."},
gaMs(){return"30 seconds, then 1 minute of breathing"},
gaMC(){return"Everything is too much and you can't even breathe calmly."},
gaMD(){return"Your heart is racing."},
gaME(){return"You need to stop right now, before you say or do something."},
gaMu(){return"Get the water ready"},
gaMt(){return"Fill a bowl or the sink with very cold water, with ice if you have it. An ice pack or something cold wrapped in a cloth also works."},
gaMw(){return'Tap "My water is ready"'},
gaMv(){return"You get 3 seconds to get there. Keep your phone nearby: you don't need to look at it."},
gaMy(){return"Face in the cold, holding your breath"},
gaMx(){return"Take a breath if you can and wet or dip your face, mostly forehead, eyes and cheeks. Halfway the phone vibrates: breathe out slowly and keep the cold on."},
gaMA(){return"Breathe slowly"},
gaMz(){return"When it ends you do a minute of slow breathing so the calm stays."},
gaMF(){return"Cold on your face while holding your breath triggers the diving reflex: your body lowers your heart rate on its own (Ackermann et al., 2023). That this helps you calm down in a bad moment comes mostly from therapists' practice, which is why you finish with a minute of slow breathing."},
gaMr(){return"If you have a heart condition, a slow pulse or take heart medication, check with a doctor first. If you feel dizzy, stop and breathe normally."},
gaMP(){return"An exercise to come back to where you are using your five senses. You name things you see, touch, hear, smell and taste, from 5 down to 1."},
gaMI(){return"2 or 3 minutes"},
gaMQ(){return"Your mind has wandered off and you can't come back."},
gaMR(){return"Everything speeds up or feels unreal."},
gaMS(){return"You have no water or room for anything else: it works anywhere."},
gaMK(){return"5 things you see"},
gaMJ(){return"Look around and name them to yourself, no writing. Tap a dot on the screen for each one."},
gaMM(){return"4 you touch and 3 you hear"},
gaML(){return"Your clothes, the chair, your hands. Then the sounds, even if they are far away or very quiet."},
gaMO(){return"2 you smell and 1 you taste"},
gaMN(){return"If you smell nothing, get close to something or recall a smell. The taste can be what your mouth tastes like now."},
gaMT(){return"To name what's around you, you have to put your attention outside, and that leaves less room for what's spinning inside. Psychologists use it a lot to come back to the present. It has little research of its own, but it's risk free and you can do it anywhere."},
gaMd(){return"A colour, a light, something you never look at. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMg(){return"Your clothes, the chair, the floor under your feet. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMc(){return"Your breathing, an engine, a distant noise. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMe(){return"The air, your clothes, something nearby. If you smell nothing, recall a smell. Tap a dot for each one."},
gaMf(){return"What your mouth tastes like now, or a sip of water. Tap the dot."},
gaNG(){return"Put what you feel into one word and say how heavy it is. It seems small, but for many people naming what they feel turns the intensity down."},
gaNz(){return"Less than 1 minute"},
gaNH(){return"You feel a lot and aren't sure what."},
gaNI(){return"Your mood is all over the place and it's hard to think."},
gaNJ(){return"You want to understand what's going on before you react."},
gaNB(){return"Pick the word"},
gaNA(){return"Among several emotions, the one that comes closest. There's no right answer."},
gaND(){return"Give it a number"},
gaNC(){return"From 1 to 10, how heavy it is right now."},
gaNF(){return"See it written"},
gaNE(){return"You see your emotion with its number. If it's heavy, we suggest breathing for a moment."},
gaNK(){return"In lab studies, naming an emotion lowered activity in the part of the brain that reacts to threats (Lieberman et al., 2007). It is called affect labeling."},
gaO0(){return"Talk to yourself by your name, the way you would talk to someone you love. Looking at it from the outside gives you distance and helps you see the situation more calmly."},
gaNU(){return"2 minutes"},
gaO1(){return"Before something that makes you tense: an interview, an exam, a talk."},
gaO2(){return"You are being hard on yourself about something."},
gaO3(){return"You keep going over the same thing and can't find a way out."},
gaNW(){return"Write what's going on"},
gaNV(){return"In one or two sentences, what's making you feel this way."},
gaNY(){return"Talk to yourself by name"},
gaNX(){return'Start with your name and go on as you would write to a friend. Use "you", not "I".'},
gaO_(){return"Read it again"},
gaNZ(){return"At the end you see your message. Read it slowly before you move on."},
gaO4(){return"Talking to yourself by name, as if to someone else, creates distance from what you feel. In studies, people who did it before public speaking saw the situation more as a challenge than a threat and felt less nervous (Kross et al., 2014)."},
gaNf(){return"A short pause to treat yourself with the same kindness you would show a friend when something goes wrong."},
gaN8(){return"1 or 2 minutes"},
gaNg(){return"Your streak broke or you didn't do what you wanted."},
gaNh(){return"Something went wrong and you are being hard on yourself."},
gaNi(){return'You feel like "it\'s ruined anyway" and want to quit.'},
gaNa(){return"Say what happened"},
gaN9(){return"In one line if you want. You can also leave it blank."},
gaNc(){return"Admit it's hard"},
gaNb(){return"A reminder that it's normal for it to hurt, and that it happens to anyone who tries something."},
gaNe(){return"Choose what you need"},
gaNd(){return"Rest, pick it up tomorrow, a small step or talk to someone. Depending on what you choose, we suggest breathing, starting for 2 minutes or talking to someone."},
gaNj(){return"Being kind to yourself after a slip isn't settling: it lowers self-criticism and rumination. Several studies of self-compassion programs pooled in a meta-analysis show it (Ferrari et al., 2019)."},
gaNu(){return"A few minutes of simple movement, no special clothes or equipment. It clears your head and can lift your energy."},
